      "id": 1,
      "text": "Queen City was laid out in May 1867 by Dr. George W. Wilson, who hoped the town would become the “Queen of the prairies.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_City%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name reflects Wilson’s ambition, and the city’s early streets were arranged around a central square surrounded by fifteen blocks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_City%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Queen City’s early economy thrived as a shipping point for railroad ties and lumber harvested from the heavily wooded areas along the Chariton River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_City%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Historic Sanborn Fire Insurance Maps from 1893, 1898, and 1910 show Queen City’s downtown layout, labeled businesses, and industrial buildings, offering a window into the town’s past.",
